const body = `<h1>1.7b — Dice Roller: Modifiers, Checks &amp; Stats</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Four more methods finish the engine. Keep running
        <a href="../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a> as you go —
        after each method, recompile and watch the <code>FAIL</code> lines shrink.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 4: roll(int num, int sides, int stat) — Add an Ability Modifier</h3>

    <p>
        Your character's strength should matter. This overload rolls the dice and then
        adds a bonus (or penalty) based on an ability score.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int num, int sides, int stat)</code></li>
        <li>Rolls <code>num</code> dice of <code>sides</code> as in Part 3, then adds
            the modifier for the ability score <code>stat</code></li>
        <li>The modifier is <code>(stat - 10) / 2</code>: a strength of 14 gives +2,
            a strength of 10 gives no change, a strength of 8 gives -1</li>
        <li>Call <code>roll(num, sides)</code> — do not repeat the loop</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            <code>stat</code> arrives as an ability score like 14, but the roll needs a
            small bonus like +2. Where does the <code>- 10</code> come from, and why
            divide by 2? Work out the modifier for a strength of 10, then 20, before
            you write any code.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("2d6 + STR(14): " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 14));  // 4 to 14
System.out.println("2d6 + STR(10): " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 10));  // 2 to 12
System.out.println("2d6 + STR(8):  " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 8));   // 1 to 11</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run each many times and check the results against the range in the comment.
        If <code>STR(14)</code> ever prints a 2, your dice are not being rolled. If
        <code>STR(8)</code> ever prints a 12, your modifier is not being applied.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Tinker: the odd-score trap.</strong> Try <code>Dice.roll(2, 6, 9)</code>.
        By the rulebook a 9 should give -1, because the true midpoint between 0 and -1
        rounds down. Java's integer division drops the remainder by moving toward zero,
        so <code>-1 / 2</code> is <code>0</code>, not <code>-1</code>. Only odd scores
        below 10 are affected: 3, 5, 7, and 9. Either accept it, or use
        <code>Math.floorDiv(stat - 10, 2)</code>, which always rounds down.</p>

    <h3>Part 5: skillCheck(int pass) — Roll a d20 Against a Difficulty</h3>

    <p>
        An ability check: roll a d20 and see whether you beat a difficulty. This method
        returns a <strong>code</strong>, not a boolean, so the caller can tell a lucky
        win from a brilliant one.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int skillCheck(int pass)</code></li>
        <li>Rolls a d20 and compares it to <code>pass</code>, the difficulty to beat</li>
    </ul>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Code</th><th>When</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Crit fail</td><td>-1</td><td>The die shows a natural 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fail</td><td>0</td><td>The roll is below <code>pass</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Success</td><td>1</td><td>The roll is at least <code>pass</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Crit success</td><td>2</td><td>The die shows a natural 20</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Check the natural 1 and natural 20 <strong>first</strong>. A natural 1 is a crit
        fail even when the difficulty was only 2, and a natural 20 is a crit success
        even when the difficulty was impossible.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("DC 15: " + Dice.skillCheck(15));
System.out.println("DC  1: " + Dice.skillCheck(1));   // only a natural 1 fails
System.out.println("DC 21: " + Dice.skillCheck(21));  // only a natural 20 passes</code></pre>
    <p>
        Watch the edges, not the middle. With DC 1 you should never see a 0. With
        DC 21 you should never see a 1.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 6: skillCheck(int pass, int skill) — Add an Ability Modifier</h3>

    <p>
        Same check, but now your character's ability score helps. You call it like
        <code>Dice.skillCheck(14, str)</code> — 14 is the difficulty of the check, and
        <code>str</code> is your strength.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int skillCheck(int pass, int skill)</code></li>
        <li>Rolls a d20, adds the modifier for the ability score <code>skill</code>,
            and compares the total to <code>pass</code></li>
        <li>Same four codes as Part 5</li>
        <li>The natural 1 and natural 20 are still read off the <em>die</em>, before
            the modifier — a +5 strength does not rescue a natural 1</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println("DC 14, STR 14 (+2): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 14));
System.out.println("DC 14, STR 10 (+0): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 10));
System.out.println("DC 14, STR 18 (+4): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 18));</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run each line twenty times. The <code>STR 18</code> line should succeed
        noticeably more often than the <code>STR 10</code> line. If all three look
        about the same, your modifier is not reaching the roll.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 7: statRoll() — Generate a Whole Set of Ability Scores</h3>

    <p>
        In many RPGs, character stats are generated by rolling 3 six-sided dice (3d6)
        and summing them. This method builds the whole set at once — the one your
        character creator will call.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public static int[] statRoll()</code></li>
        <li>Returns a <strong>new array</strong> of length 6</li>
        <li>Each element is a 3d6 roll, so each one is between 3 and 18</li>
        <li>The six scores are STR, DEX, CON, INT, WIS, CHA, in that order</li>
        <li>Call <code>roll(3, 6)</code> for each element — do not repeat the loop
            or the formula</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge</h3>
        <p>
            Why call <code>roll(3, 6)</code> once per element instead of writing
            <code>(int)(Math.random() * 6) + 1</code> three times inside the loop?
            What happens if you later discover a bug in your random formula — how many
            places would you need to fix?
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>int[] stats = Dice.statRoll();
String[] names = {"STR", "DEX", "CON", "INT", "WIS", "CHA"};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; stats.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(names[i] + ": " + stats[i]);
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Expected: six labeled numbers, each between 3 and 18. Run it several times —
        do the numbers move around? If every stat comes out the same, you are filling
        the array with one roll instead of rolling per index.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Turn an ability score into a modifier with <code>(stat - 10) / 2</code></li>
            <li>Return a code instead of a boolean when there are more than two outcomes</li>
            <li>Test a method's edge cases rather than its typical case</li>
            <li>Build and return an array, filling it one index at a time</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

